speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, under the provisions of Standing Order 43, I ask the unanimous consent of the House to discuss an important matter of pressing necessity.
Considering the incredible statement just made by Quebec minister Claude Simard that although they are highly contaminated by PCB and unfit for consumption, one should not worry since eels taken from the St. Lawrence are 97 per cent sold on export markets; and considering that this poor specimen of humanity was echoing similar statements by two federal ministers-including the present Minister of State for Fisheries-despite the fact that serious reports indicate a PCB rate averaging eight times higher than the maximum security standard allowed by the Department of National Health and Welfare, I move, seconded by the hon. member for Roberval (Mr. Gauthier);
That the House now discuss the entire matter and review the duties and responsibilities of the authorities as regards pollution and health to avoid on one hand that Canada and Quebec be accused of poisoning foreign countries and on the other hand to avoid that the Canadian people lose further confidence in their leaders since they can no longer rely on food security standards within the country.

Mr. Speaker, I rise on a matter of urgent and pressing necessity pursuant to the provisions of Standing Order 43.
Considering that bilingualism in air communications is an essential matter that will determine to what extent Francophones can put any confidence in the future of bilingualism in Canada and considering that the Minister of Transport (Mr. Lang) said himself how impressed he was by bilingualism in air-to-ground communications in Paris, which proves that bilingualism is also possible and logical in air-to-ground communications in Quebec, I move, seconded by the hon. member for Richmond (Mr. Beaudoin);
That this House call on the government to establish immediately and without delay a policy of bilingualism in air communications, since it has been proven that in this field it is possible and logical to respect the policy of bilingualism which is essential to the survival of Canada.
